      "source_text": "Intuition and example. In one dimension fixed points are where the graph of $g$ crosses the diagonal $y=x$; the iteration walks a staircase (\"cobweb\") between graph and diagonal, which a contraction pulls into the crossing (see the explorer). For $g(x)=0.5x+1$, solving $x=0.5x+1$ gives $x^\\ast=2$. From $x_0=0$: $x_1=1$, $x_2=1.5$, $x_3=1.75$, $x_4=1.875$; the errors $2,1,0.5,0.25,0.125$ halve because $|g(x)-g(y)|=0.5|x-y|$. This is value iteration $V_{k+1}=1+\\gamma V_k$ for one state with reward $1$ and $\\gamma=0.5$: the iterates are the partial sums $1+\\gamma+\\dots+\\gamma^{k-1}$ of Section 4, with limit $1/(1-\\gamma)$.",
